WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:02.570
- [Narrator] With air travel inching back

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.610
from its collapse during the pandemic,

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.420
Americans might be able to
fly to Europe this summer.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.510
That is if you're vaccinated.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.570
Exactly how this new era of
pandemic travel will work

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.060
is still up in the air

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.890
but there's one approach a lot
of people are talking about.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.220
- Vaccine passports.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.472
- COVID passports.

00:00:19.472 --> 00:00:20.765
- [Reporter] Vaccines passports.

00:00:20.765 --> 00:00:22.300
- [Reporter] A COVID passport.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.080
- [Narrator] The so-called
vaccine passports

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.170
or digital health pass systems aim

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.880
to revive the tourism industry,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.090
allow people to attend concerts and events

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.800
and get back to the office.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.010
But creating a coordinated
health pass system

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.360
is so fraught with logistical

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.680
and ethical conflicts that it
could take years to pull off.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.440
Startups, nonprofits and governments

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.990
have already rolled out different kinds

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:43.380
of digital health passes,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.610
from Israel's Green Pass

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.460
to an app that allows access
to holy sites in Mecca.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.530
But so far, there's no single pass

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.710
that's used all across the world.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.550
So what would it take
for digital health passes

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.170
to get people back in the air again

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.623
and out in the world?

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.570
- This is really an
unprecedented situation

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:07.670
in the modern global economy.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:09.620
- [Narrator] Julie Wernau
reports on the impact

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.080
of COVID-19 on business for
The Wall Street Journal.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.130
- Really the goal of these systems

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.230
is to make people feel safe enough

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:22.320
to resume normal, everyday activities.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.620
Everything from eating
inside at a restaurant,

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.860
or going to a concert venue or traveling.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.210
- [Narrator] The idea is to
have an official digital record

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.750
of your COVID history
that when you travel,

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.420
authorities can check and
determine how likely it is

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.480
that you're carrying the virus.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.220
The big challenge is making something

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.040
that's accepted across borders

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.090
and stays updated with other
countries' COVID regulations.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.380
Several groups, including
privacy advocates,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.070
businesses and healthcare providers

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.750
have proposed solutions.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.640
One of those stakeholders
is Dakota Gruener.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.114
She's leading a coalition
of over 150 partners,

00:01:59.114 --> 00:02:02.090
including all the major
health pass providers,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.100
to create a set of global standards

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.810
to underpin digital health passes.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.890
She says there are a few main features

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.290
every digital health pass should have.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.990
The first is something
called interoperability,

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:14.860
meaning it should work

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.310
across different institutions and borders.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:21.090
- A person can be tested or vaccinated

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.890
wherever it is that they live.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.890
And get to an airport or get to a border

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.060
and have that pass be seamlessly
recognized and trusted.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.820
- [Narrator] Say you're traveling
from New York to London.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.960
A successful app would tell
you the latest guidelines

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.180
for both cities and integrate

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.460
your official COVID health
records from each country.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.060
Airlines are trying out different options,

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.720
like CommonPass, VeriFLY
and IATA Travel Pass.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.610
But most trials are
limited to select routes

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.390
and passengers aren't
required to use the pass.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:59.560
So it's hard to tell how it
would work on a mass scale.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.923
- The reality is that so far,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.930
we're not really seeing this

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.600
in a very mandatory way very many places

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.290
because people don't even know

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.440
how to mandate it

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.580
and they don't know what
counts as proof or evidence

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.320
that you've been vaccinated.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.600
- [Narrator] Also key to health passes,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:19.640
according to Gruener,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.640
are verifiable health records.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.820
These include things like
vaccine certificates,

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.480
as well as pass COVID test
results in past cases.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.120
- If you can have both proof of testing

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.100
or proof of vaccination,

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.783
that allows people flexibility.

00:03:33.583 --> 00:03:35.141
If they can't be vaccinated,

00:03:35.141 --> 00:03:36.930
perhaps they can be tested.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.360
- [Narrator] In a digital
health pass system,

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:39.950
health records would need to be uploaded

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.390
to the app and accessible
to different authorities,

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.440
like physicians or border
agents for different uses.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.580
Experts say consumers should be in control

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.073
of how much health data
they share and with whom.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.330
For example, you might choose

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.690
to share more health information

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.270
with a doctor giving you
your second vaccine dose

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.570
and less data with the airline
you're checking in with

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.260
at the airport.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.240
- We think that this idea

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.431
of selective disclosure
is really important

00:04:03.431 --> 00:04:04.410
because it would allow me

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.520
to share just the minimum
information necessary.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.420
- [Narrator] When that
information is shared,

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.860
authorities need to know
that they can trust it.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.910
That's where another key factor comes in,

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:15.870
verifiable credentials.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:19.270
- And that is literally a packet of data

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:22.060
that is verified,

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.250
that you can say this has
been signed and sealed

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:28.020
and I know the packet of data

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.680
has not been tampered with

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.682
and I know who it was who's attesting

00:04:31.682 --> 00:04:34.100
to this packet of data.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.670
- [Narrator] But in order
for digital health passes

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.232
to be embraced by both
government and regular people,

00:04:38.232 --> 00:04:41.860
they need to address the ongoing
debate over data privacy.

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:44.500
- In America, privacy and health privacy

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.870
has always been a huge hot button issue

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:52.010
and people are concerned
about sharing even that amount

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:53.680
of information and want to know

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:55.620
that that record is safe.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.180
- [Narrator] Some groups
have been advocating

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.150
for centralizing health
data since before COVID-19.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:02.790
But resistance to sharing this data

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.090
has extended to the current pandemic.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.930
Several states, including Texas,

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.410
have banned having to
show proof of vaccination,

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.550
including vaccine passports.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:15.450
- Government should not require any Texan

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.800
to show proof of vaccination

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.140
and reveal private health information

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:22.000
just to go about their daily lives.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.400
- [Narrator] Cybersecurity experts

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.060
and Israel's Public Health Union

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.390
have raised concerns about
sharing sensitive health data.

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:29.990
- This is a messy ecosystem.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.870
There're a huge number
of potential issuers

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:34.680
of a health pass.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.760
Testing centers, vaccinators, states

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.916
and also a huge number
of potential verifiers.

00:05:40.916 --> 00:05:45.460
The airlines, border control agencies.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:46.880
- [Narrator] Workplaces and employers

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.600
are also considering
tracking health records.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.060
One MERCER survey found

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.260
that almost 51% of companies

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:54.700
are either currently tracking

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:56.790
their employees' vaccination status

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.670
or are considering it.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.360
While individual states are
taking different approaches

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.740
to regulating vaccine passports,

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.570
big tech is laying out some guidelines

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:07.760
for COVID-related apps too.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.430
Apple has said COVID-19
app developers need

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.290
to work with recognized government

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:15.560
or health authorities
before submitting to Apple.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.950
And Google has a list of
additional requirements

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:21.060
for COVID-19 apps going
into its Play Store.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:24.420
Still because there are so
many apps out there right now,

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:25.930
the exact privacy policy

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:28.220
could vary slightly from each app.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.090
What's more, some health officials contend

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.690
that tech companies are over
complicating the process.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.470
And this health
information could be stored

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.710
in a much simpler form, like in a QR code.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.090
And there are bigger
questions about how these apps

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:43.060
will be used together.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:45.140
Some apps, like the IATA Travel Pass

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.840
and VeriFLY are focused
on the travel industry,

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.030
while others like Israel's Green Pass

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:51.772
or New York's Excelsior Pass

00:06:51.772 --> 00:06:54.040
are being used to grant people access

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.020
to events or spaces.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.700
- The concern that
essentially by asking people

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.680
to prove that they've been vaccinated

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.740
in order to do very normal things

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.150
like go to work or attend a concert,

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.480
that we're creating a system

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.750
in which there are two classes of people,

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.160
the vaccinated and the unvaccinated.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.224
- Policy is as important as the technology

00:07:18.224 --> 00:07:21.610
and we need policymakers
to define the range

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:23.570
of acceptable use cases

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:28.570
and specifically, define what
is not an acceptable use case

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:30.690
for a health pass.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.620
- [Narrator] Exactly where that line is

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.850
is still being worked out

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:36.150
and finding that balance

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.760
is going to be crucial
for international travel,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.381
business and public health.

00:07:40.381 --> 00:07:43.131
(dramatic music)

